\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Unobserved Effects Linear Panel Data Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Wooldridge Slides
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We already covered panel data models where the error term had no particular
 structure.
 But we assumed either contemporaneous exogeneity (POLS) or strict exogeneity
 (FGLS).
 Now, we explicitly add a time constant, 
\emph on
unobserved effect
\emph default
 to the model, often called 
\emph on
unobserved heterogeneity
\series bold
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Start with the balanced panel case, and assume random sampling across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (the cross section dimension), with fixed time period 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{it},y_{it}):t=1,\ldots,T,c_{i}\right\} $
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the unobserved effect drawn along with the observed data.
 The unbalanced case is trickier because we must know why we are missing
 some time periods for some units.
 We consider this much later under missing/data sample selection issues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from the population, the basic model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 are the 
\emph on
idiosyncratic errors.

\emph default
 The 
\emph on
composite error
\emph default
 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{it}=c_{i}+u_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ v_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 is almost certainly serially correlated, and definitely is if 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}\}$
\end_inset

 is serially uncorrelated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is useful to write a population version of the model in conditional expectati
on form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta_{j}=\frac{\partial E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})}{\partial x_{itj}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{itj}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

, so that we are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

holding 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 fixed.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Our hope is that we can allow 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With a single cross section, there is nothing we can do unless we can find
 good observable IVs for the endogenous elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 But with two or more periods, we have more options.
 We can write the population model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{it} & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}\\
E(u_{it}|x_{it},c_{i}) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Suppose we have 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

 time periods:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i1} & = & x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{i1}\\
y_{i2} & = & x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{i2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Subtract 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2$
\end_inset

 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y_{i}=y_{i2}-y_{i1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{i}=x_{i2}-x_{i1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u_{i}=u_{i2}-u_{i1}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y_{i}=\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

which is now a cross section in the changes or differences.
 Sufficient for OLS on a random sample to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(\Delta x_{i}\Delta u_{i}) & = & 0\\
\mbox{rank}\ E(\Delta x_{i}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}) & = & K
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The rank condition is violated if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 has elements that do not change over time.
 Assume each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 has some time variation (that is, for at least some members in the population).
 The orthogonality condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})(u_{i2}-u_{i1})\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

But
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[(x_{i2}-x_{i1})(u_{i2}-u_{i1})\right] & = & E(x_{i2}u_{i2})-E(x_{i1}u_{i2})-E(x_{i2}u_{i1})+E(x_{i1}u_{i1})\\
 & = & -\left[E(x_{i1}u_{i2})+E(x_{i2}u_{i1})\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 under the conditional mean specification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

OLS on the differences will only be consistent if we add
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{is}u_{it})=0,\ s\neq t
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is a kind of strict exogeneity assumption.
 However, we have removed 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 from the composit error.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is weaker than assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with the composit error, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 It is very unlikely that we omit an intercept from the differenced equation.
 If we start with a model with different intercepts,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i1} & = & \theta_{i1}+x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{i1}\\
y_{i2} & = & \theta_{i2}+x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{i2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y_{i}=\alpha_{i}+\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}=\theta_{i2}-\theta_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is the change in the aggregate time effects (intercepts).
 Now the rank condition also excludes variables that change by the same
 amount for each unit (such as age).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As mentioned earlier, we assume a balanced panel and all asymptotic analysis,
 implicit or explicit, is with fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the size of the cross section.
 The basic 
\emph on
unobserved effects model
\emph default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 and so is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 In addition to unobserved effect and unobserved heterogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is sometimes called a 
\emph on
latent effect
\emph default
 or an individual effect, firm effect, school effect, and so on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An extension of the basic model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\eta_{t}c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \eta_{t}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 are unknown parameters (and we have to assume something like 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{1}=1$
\end_inset

).
 More on this later.
 As in the earlier treatment, the model is written with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 not depending on time.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 can include time period dummies and interactions of variables with time
 periods dummies, so the model is quite flexible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A general specfication is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=g_{t}^{\prime}\theta+z_{i}^{\prime}\delta+w_{it}^{\prime}\gamma+c_{i}+u_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of aggregate time effects (often time dummies), 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a set of time-constant observed variables, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}$
\end_inset

 changes across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (for at least some units 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and time periods 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}$
\end_inset

 can include interactions among time-constant and time varying variables.
 In microeconometric applications, it is best to avoid calling 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

random effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixed effect.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We are always treating 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as a random variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Assumptions about the Unobserved Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In modern applications, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

random effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 essentially means
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(x_{it},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

although we often will stregthen this.
 The term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixed effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means that no restricitions are placed on the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Recently, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

correlated random effects
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is used to denot situations where we model the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and it is especially useful for nonlinear models (but also for linear
 models).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Exogeneity Assumptions on the Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Contemporaneous Exogeneity Conditional on the Unobserved Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Ideally, we could proceed with just this assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Strict Exogeneity Conditional on the Unobserved Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that only 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 affects the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 once 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is controlled for.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is weaker than if we did not condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming the condition holds conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+E(c_{i}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})
\]

\end_inset

So correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})$
\end_inset

 would invalidate the assumption without conditioning on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

But strict exogeneity conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 rules out lagged dependent variables and feedback.
 Written in terms of the idiosyncratic errors, strict exogeneity is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t+h}$
\end_inset

 must be uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $h>0$
\end_inset

.
 In addition to ruling out feedback, strict exogeneity assumes we have any
 distributed lag dynamics correct, too.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}=(z_{it},z_{i,t-1})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|z_{i1},\ldots,z_{it},\ldots,z_{iT},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|z_{it},z_{i,t-1},c_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Sequential Exogeneity Conditional on the Unobserved Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A more reasonable assumption that we will use later is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{it},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,x_{i1},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Sequential exogeneity assumes correct distributed lag dynamics but is silent
 on feedback.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Estimation and Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are four common methods: POLS, random effects, fixed effects, and
 first differencing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
POLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We already covered this.
 Now, we just recognize that the equation is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{it} & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+v_{it}\\
v_{it} & = & c_{i}+u_{it}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Consistency (fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

) of the POLS estimator is ensured by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(x_{it}c_{i}) & = & 0\\
E(x_{it}u_{it}) & = & 0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Contemporaneous exogeneity is weaker than strict exogeneity, but it buys
 us little in practice because POLS also uses 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, which cannot hold for lagged dependent variables and is unlikely for other
 varaibles not strictly exogenous.
 Inference should be made robust to serial correlation and heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{it}=y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{POLS}$
\end_inset

 be the POLS residuals.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{POLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}\hat{v}_{it}\hat{v_{is}}x_{it}x_{its}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or sometimes with an adjustment, such as multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{N}{(N-1)}$
\end_inset

.
 We can also write this estimator as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{POLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{v}_{i}\hat{v}_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Random Effects Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We will state assumptions in conditional mean terms so that second moment
 derivations are easier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
RE.1:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})=E(c_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 includes (at least) unity, and probably time dummies in addition.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 without loss of generality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A GLS approach also leaves 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the error term:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+v_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

and we know the properties of FGLS when
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{is}v_{it})=0,\ \forall\ s,t=1,\ldots T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This weaker version of strict exogeneity is implied by Assumption RE.1.
 Write the equaiton in system form (for all time periods) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+v_{i}+c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of all ones.
 Define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega_{T\times T}=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})=\mbox{Var}(v_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
RE.2:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

RE imposes a special structure on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 (which could be wrong).
 Under RE.1(a), 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated.
 Assume further that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{Var}(u_{it}) & = & \sigma_{u}^{2},\ t=1,\ldots,T\\
\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is}) & = & 0,\ t\neq s
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{Var}(v_{it}) & = & \mbox{Var}(c_{i}+u_{it})\nonumber \\
 & = & \mbox{Var}(c_{i})+\mbox{Var}(u_{it})\nonumber \\
\sigma_{v}^{2} & = & \sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Further, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{Cov}(v_{it},v_{is}) & = & \mbox{Cov}(c_{i}+u_{it},c_{i}+u_{is})\nonumber \\
 & = & \mbox{Var}(c_{i})+\mbox{Cov}(c_{i},u_{is})+\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},c_{i})+\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is})\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{c}^{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

This leads to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

random effects
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exchangeable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 structure for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Omega & = & E\left[(c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i})(c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i})^{\prime}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & E(c_{i}^{2})j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}+E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{c}^{2}j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}+\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2} & \sigma_{c}^{2} & \cdots & \sigma_{c}^{2}\\
\sigma_{c}^{2} & \sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2} & \cdots & \sigma_{c}^{2}\\
\vdots &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{c}^{2} & \cdots & \sigma_{c}^{2} & \sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix depends on only two parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, or, more directly, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Feasible GLS requires estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, that is, the two parameters.
 Actually, it would be enough to know 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\nicefrac{\sigma_{c}^{2}}{\left(\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}\right)}$
\end_inset

, the fraction of the total variance accounted for by 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\mbox{Corr}(v_{it},v_{is})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

.
 We can also write 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega=\sigma_{v}^{2}\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
1 & \rho & \cdots & \rho\\
\rho & 1 & \cdots & \rho\\
\vdots &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
\rho & \cdots & \rho & 1
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which shows we only need to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 to proceed with FGLS.
 Typically, we estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is useful for summarizing the importance of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can use POLS to get residuals, 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{v}_{it}$
\end_inset

, across all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Then a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 (not generally unbiased), as 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 gets large for fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=(NT-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\check{v}_{it}^{2}=\nicefrac{SSR}{(NT-K)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the usual variance estimator from OLS regression.
 This is based on, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(v_{it}^{2})$
\end_inset

 and then average across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, too.
 Then replace population with sample average, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 with POLS estimates, and subtract 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 as a degrees-of-freedom adjustment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

, note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{c}^{2}=\left[\nicefrac{T(T-1)}{2}\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T-1}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=t+1}^{T}}E(v_{it}v_{is})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So a consistent 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

estimator
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\left[\nicefrac{NT(T-1)}{2}\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T-1}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=t+1}^{T}}v_{it}v_{is}
\]

\end_inset

An actual estimator replaces 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 with the POLS residuals,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\left[\frac{NT(T-1)}{2}-K\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T-1}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=t+1}^{T}}\check{v}_{it}\check{v}_{is}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and subtracts 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{NT(T-1)}{2}$
\end_inset

 as a degrees-of-freedom adjustment.
 By the usual argument,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{p}{\displaystyle \lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}}\ \hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\sigma_{c}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we can use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2} & \hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2} & \cdots & \hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}\\
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2} & \hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2} & \cdots & \hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}\\
\vdots &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2} & \cdots & \hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2} & \hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Lambda}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
1 & \hat{\rho} & \cdots & \hat{\rho}\\
\hat{\rho} & 1 &  & \hat{\rho}\\
\vdots &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
\hat{\rho} & \cdots & \hat{\rho} & 1
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}=\nicefrac{\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}}{\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}}$
\end_inset

 in FGLS.
 It is possible for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 to be negative, which means the basic unboserved effects variance-covariance
 structure is faulty.
 Typically, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

 unless the variables have been transformed in some way, such as being first
 differenced, before apply GLS.
 The FGLS estimator that uses this particular structure of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
random effects (RE) estimator
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Fully robust inference is available for RE, and there are good reasons for
 doing so.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 may not have the special (and restricitive, especially for large 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

) RE structure, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 need not have the RE form.
 Serial correlation or changing variances in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 invalidate the RE structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The system homoskedasticity requirement,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

might not hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fully robust estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{RE})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\hat{v}_{i}\hat{v}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}_{RE}$
\end_inset

 is the vector of RE (FGLS) residuals.
 Sometimes, an iterative procedure is used.
 These new residuals can be used to obtain a new estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, and so on.
 For first order asymptotics, there is no efficiency gain from iterating.
 Though, it might help with smaller 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the advantage of RE, which imposes specific assumptions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, and the unrestricted FGLS we discussed earlier? Theoretically, nothing.
 We do not get more efficiency with large 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and small 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 by imposing restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 If system homoskedasticity holds but 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is not of the RE form, an unrestricted FGLS analysis is more efficient
 than RE (again, fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

).
 RE does have some appeal because of its implicit transformation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A nonrobust variance matrix estimator can be used if we add an assumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
RE.3:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}^{2}|x_{i})=\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumptions RE.1 and RE.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 has the RE structure and system homoskedasticity holds.
 Part (a) is homoskedasticity and serial uncorrelatedness of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

, and (b) is homoskedasticity of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under RE.1, RE.2, and RE.3
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{RE})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}x_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is a valid estimator.
 Inference is straightforward.
 Typically, we use a Wald or a robust Wald statistic for multiple restrictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Fixed Effects Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unlike POLS and RE, fixed effects estimation removes 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to form an estimating equation.
 We average the original equation, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to get a cross-sectional equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{y}_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the overbar indiciates 
\emph on
time averages
\emph default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{y}_{i}=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}y_{it},\ \bar{x}_{i}=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it},\ \bar{u}_{i}=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

The equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is often called the 
\emph on
between equation
\emph default
 because it relies on variation in the data between cross seciton observations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\emph on
between estimator
\emph default
 is the OLS estimator form the cross section regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{y}_{i}\ \mbox{on}\ \bar{x}_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset

In practice, an intercept is included to account for nonzero 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The between estimator is inconsistent unless
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Cov}(\bar{x}_{i},c_{i})=0,\ \mbox{Cov}(\bar{x}_{i},\bar{u}_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instead, subtract off the time-averaged equation from the original equation
 to eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i}=(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{it}=\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{it}=y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and so on.
 We call this the 
\emph on
time demeaned equation
\emph default
, and the transformation is 
\emph on
time demeaning
\emph default
, 
\emph on
fixed effects
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
within
\emph default
 (time variation within each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is used).
 The key is that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is gone from the time demeaned equation.
 So we can use POLS:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{it}\ \mbox{on}\ \ddot{x}_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T;\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is the 
\emph on
fixed effects (FE) estimator
\emph default
 or the 
\emph on
within estimator
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{FE} & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{y}_{it}\right)\\
 & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}y_{it}\right)\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})(y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the weakest orthogonality assumption for consistency? We can just
 apply the results for POLS, but it is useful to see it directly.
 Write the estimator by substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{it}=\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{FE} & = & \beta+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{u}_{it}\right)\\
 & = & \beta+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}u_{it}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

By the WLLN, as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 with fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, the key moment condition for consistency is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{x}_{it}u_{it})={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E\left[(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})u_{it}\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition to contemporaneous exogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, we need a kind of strict exogeneity
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\bar{x}_{i}u_{it})=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}E(x_{is}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FE.1:
\series default
 Same as RE.1(a), that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape up
This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{is}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{x}_{it}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 The rank condition is directly from POLS.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FE.2:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ \left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime})\right]=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rank condition rules out elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 that have no time variation for any unit in the population.
 Such variables get swept away by the within transformation.
 Under FE.1 and FE.2
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{FE}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\beta\ \mbox{as}\ N\rightarrow\infty
\]

\end_inset

The FE estimator works well for large 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, too, but showing that requires putting restrictions on the time series
 process 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{it},y_{it}):t=1,\ldots\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What parameters can we identify with FE? Suppose we start with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=\theta_{1}+\theta_{2}d2_{t}+\cdots+\theta_{T}dT_{t}+z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{1}+d2_{t}z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{2}+\cdots+DT_{t}z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{T}+w_{it}^{\prime}\delta+c_{i}+u_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using FE, we cannot estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

, but all other parameters are generally identified.
 FE allows 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be arbitrarily correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{i},w_{it})$
\end_inset

, and so we cannot distinguish 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}+z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We can estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{2},\ldots,\theta_{T}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2},\ldots,\gamma_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 So we can estimate whether the effect of the time constant variables has
 changed over time.
 We cannot estimate the effect in any period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 because it is 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}+\gamma_{t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can obtain a variance matrix estimator valid under Assumptions FE.1 and
 FE.2.
 Define the FE residuals as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}=\ddot{y}_{it}-\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ t=1,\ldots,T;\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

These are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

estimates
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

, not the 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 This has implications for estimating the error variance, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Without additional assumptions, use the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

cluster-robust
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{is}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Of course, a nonrobust form requires an extra assumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FE.3:
\series default
 Same as RE.3(a), that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape up
To find the asymptotic variance under this assumption, remember the general
 form for a POLS estimator, in this case, on the time demeaned data, is
 the sandwich form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}E\left(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

Under FE.3, we can simplify the middle matrix.
 First, use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{u}_{it}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}u_{it}=\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right) & = & E\left(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left[E\left(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)|\ddot{x}_{i}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left[\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}E\left(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i}\right)\ddot{x}_{i}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left[\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}(\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T})\ddot{x}_{i}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

 under FE.3.
 So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta)\right]=\sigma_{u}^{2}\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under FE.1, FE.2, and FE.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 requires some care because we effectively observe 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

, not 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Under the constant variance and no serial correlation assumptions on 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{Var}(\ddot{u}_{it}) & = & \mbox{Var}(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}+\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}-2\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},\bar{u}_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}+\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}-2\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{T}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=(T-1)\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

One degree of freedom is lost for each unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 because of the time demeaning: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ddot{u}_{it}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{u}^{2}=\left[N(T-1)\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and now take away expectation, insert 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and use a degree-of-freedom adjustment to account for estimating the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\left[N(T-1)-K\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}^{2}=\frac{SSR}{N(T-1)-K}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is actually unbiased under FE.1, FE.2, and FE.3.
 It is consistent as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Under FE.1, FE.2, and FE.3,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}=\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}\left(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and this is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 asymptotic variance estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If you do the time-demeaning and run POLS, the usual statistics do not reflect
 the lost degress of freedom (
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 of them).
 The estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{SSR}{(NT-K)}$
\end_inset

, which is too small.
 Canned FE packages compute the statistics.
 The FE estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 can also be obtained by running a long regression on the original data,
 and including dummy variables for each cross section unit:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}\ \mbox{on}\ d1_{i},d2_{i},\ldots,dN_{i},x_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T;\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

often called the 
\emph on
dummy variable regression
\emph default
.
 The statistics are properly computed because the inclusion of the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 dummy variables.
 The only danger is treating the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as parameters to estimate, while sensible with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

large
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, it can lead to trouble later with nonlinear models.
 Here, we get a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 for fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes, we want to estimate the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 using the 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods.
 We do not have to run the dummy variable regression.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{c}_{i}=\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

With small 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, this is not a good 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

estimate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, but it is unbiased.
 We can estimate features of the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 well:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\mu}_{c} & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{c}_{i}\\
 & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}(\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE})\\
 & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left[c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}(\beta-\hat{\beta}_{FE})\right]\\
 & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}c_{i}+N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\bar{u}_{i}+\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\bar{x}_{i}\right)\left(\beta-\hat{\beta}_{FE}\right)\\
 & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}c_{i}+o_{p}(1)+O_{p}(1)o_{p}(1)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\mu_{c}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Stata, for example, reports 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{c}$
\end_inset

 as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intercept
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

constant
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in FE regressions.
 This consistency argument uses only FE.1 and FE.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can estimate other features of the distribution, too, although some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

obvious
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 estimators are inconsistent.
 For example, we might try to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 using the sample variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \hat{c}_{i}:i=1,\ldots,N\right\} $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=(N-1)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}(\hat{c}_{i}-\hat{\mu}_{c})^{2}
\]

\end_inset

But under FE.1 to FE.3 it can be shown that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{p\lim}(\tilde{\sigma}_{c}^{2})=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\mbox{Var}(\bar{u}_{i})=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}
\]

\end_inset

We can adjust for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bias
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 using the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\tilde{\sigma}_{c}^{2}-\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}}{T}=(N-1)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}(\hat{c}_{i}-\hat{\mu}_{c})^{2}-\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}}{T}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 If we treat the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as parameters, we can test the null that they are the same.
 This is easy to see if we add to FE.1 to FE.3 the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Then the classical linear model assumptions hold, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:c_{1}=c_{2}=\cdots=c_{N}$
\end_inset

 can be tested using an 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 statistic with 
\begin_inset Formula $N-1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N(T-1)-K$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also obtain an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 using
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{c}^{2}=\sigma_{v}^{2}-\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We alread have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\nicefrac{SSR}{\left[N(T-1)-K\right]}$
\end_inset

, which is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 under FE.1 to FE.3.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

 and so a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=(NT-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}(y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\hat{\mu}_{v})^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{v}$
\end_inset

 is the sample average of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Fixed Effects GLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Write the 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods for a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and let the variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 unrestricted matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

.
 When we eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 by demeaning we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{i}=\ddot{x}_{i}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where, for example, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}=I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric, idempotent with rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}=0$
\end_inset

, we know the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has rank less than 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, (unconditional) variance covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega\equiv E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})=E(Q_{t}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}Q_{T})=Q_{T}\Lambda Q_{T}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 Applying FGLS to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ddot{y}_{i}=\ddot{x}_{i}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

is tricky because the generalized inverse is required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is a simple solution.
 After demeaning to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 using all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods and obtaining
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

drop one of the time periods.
 It doe not matter which one is dropped (but the first or last are easiest).
 Apply FGLS to the 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

 remaining equations using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

.
 Remember, we can still make a case for robust inference because system
 heteroskedasticity is always a possibility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
First-Differencing Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Like FE, FD removes 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 But it does it by differencing adjacent observations.
 FE and FD are the same when 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

, but differe otherwise.
 Again, start with the orginial equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

For FD, we explicitly lose the first time period
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{it}=\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The FD estimator is POLS on the first differences.
 In practice, we might not difference period dummies, unless we are interested
 in the year intercepts in the original levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

FD also requires a kind of strict exogeneity.
 The weakest assumption is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\Delta x_{it}\Delta u_{it})=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Failure of strict exogeneity will cause different inconsistencies in FE
 and FD when 
\begin_inset Formula $T>2$
\end_inset

.
 In unbalanced cases, FD requires that data exists in adjacent time periods.
 FE does not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A sufficient condition is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FD.1:
\series default
 
\emph on
Same as FE.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also need the following rank condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FD.2:
\series default
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix with rows 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i})=K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This should make inference robust to serial correlation and heteroskedasticity
 in the differences errors, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{it}\equiv u_{it}-u_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After POLS on the first differences, let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{e}_{it}=\Delta y_{it}-\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FD},\ t=2,\ldots T;\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{e}_{i}=(\hat{e}_{i2},\ldots,\hat{e}_{iT})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 residuals.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FD})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{e}_{i}\hat{e}_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the fully robust variance matrix estimator.
 Use POLS on the first differences, and then use a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

cluster
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also add a homoskedasticity assumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
FD.3:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(e_{i}e_{i}^{\prime}|\Delta x_{i})=\sigma_{e}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}=E(e_{it}^{2})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumption FE.3, the usual POLS statistics in the FD regression are
 asymptotically valid.
 If we believe FD.3, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}=u_{i,t-1}+e_{it}$
\end_inset

 is a random walk.
 In a pure time series setting, this means the regression would be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spurious.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 For a given 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the time series 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{it} & = & c_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}\\
u_{it} & = & u_{i,t-1}+e_{it}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the intercept for unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 This doe not define a sensible time series regression because 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mean reverting.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 One way to see this is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{it})=\sigma_{e}^{2}t$
\end_inset

, and so the idiosyncratic error variance grows as a linear function of
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Here, we can allow random walk behavior in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}\right\} $
\end_inset

 with a short 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 because we have cross section variation driving the large-sample analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Hausman and Taylor Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

IV methods for FE and FD require us to find IVs from outside the model.
 This is often difficult (just as in the cross section case).
 Hausman and Taylor (1981) proposed assuming that certain variables are
 appropriately exogenous, and using these as IVs.
 Write the HT model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=w_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 are time-constant variables (including an intercept) and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 are time-varying variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We maintain the strict exogeneity assumption condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, RE.1(a):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If we want to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, we can just use FE without furhter assumptions.
 What about estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

? Suppose we 
\emph on
assume
\emph default
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(w_{i}c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{i}-w_{i}^{\prime}\gamma-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta=c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[w_{i}\left(\bar{y}_{i}-w_{i}\gamma-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(w_{i}w_{i}^{\prime})\gamma=E\left[w_{i}(\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So, a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\gamma}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}w_{i}w_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}w_{i}\left(\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the OLS coefficients from the cross section regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}\ \mbox{on}\ w_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In practice, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 would contain a set of year dummies and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 would contain an overal intercept (which allows the mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be different from zero).
 Computing standard errors for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 must account for estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

.
 We could stack the two sets of moment conditions and use system IV.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now analyze the model under more general assumptions.
 First, partition 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=(w_{i1},w_{i2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}=(x_{it1},x_{it2})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Then assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(w_{i1}c_{i}) & = & 0\\
E(x_{it1}c_{i}) & = & 0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Write the system as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=w_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+x_{i}\beta+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 demeaning matrix.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}x_{i}$
\end_inset

, is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix with rows 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, is a valid set of instruments because 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}j_{T}=0$
\end_inset

 and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(Q_{T}x_{i}\right)^{\prime}v_{i}=\left(Q_{T}x_{i}\right)^{\prime}u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\left(Q_{T}x_{i}\right)^{\prime}u_{i}\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumptions given, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 can act as its own IVs.
 But we need instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 If we define 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}=(x_{i11},x_{i21},\ldots x_{iT1})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i1}^{o}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

; technically, we can use all of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

 as IVs in each time period.
 Then the matrix of IVs is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[Q_{T}x_{i},j_{T}\otimes(w_{i1},x_{i1}^{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

But, it is probably misleading to think all of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

 has explanatory power for the time-constant 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, under certain assumptions (i.i.d.
 is sufficient, but not necessary), 
\begin_inset Formula $L(w_{i2}|x_{i1}^{o})=L(w_{i2}|\bar{x}_{i1})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of time averages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}\geq J_{2}$
\end_inset

, we might only use 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i1}$
\end_inset

.
 (If 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}<J_{2}$
\end_inset

 the entire strategy is questionable.) In other words, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 use IVs
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(\ddot{x}_{it},w_{i1},\bar{x}_{i1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and use REIV.
 (Again, we should make the inference robust.) Given the instruments, we
 can use GMM-3SLS with unrestricted 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 or even optimal GMM with a general weighting matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The bottom line is that, if we ahve enough time-varying variables that we
 think are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 (as well as being strictly exogenous with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

), then we can allow a subset of the time-constant variables to be correlated
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Estimation Under Sequential Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider IV estimation of the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

under the sequential exogeneity assumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{it},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,x_{i1},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Actually, for consistency, we can get by with the weaker form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(x_{is},u_{it})=0,\ \forall\ s\leq t
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This leads to simple moment conditions after first differencing:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Delta y_{it} & = & \Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T\\
E(x_{is}\Delta u_{it}) & = & 0,\ s=1,\ldots,t-1;\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Therefore, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the available instruments in the FD equation are in the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}^{o}\equiv(x_{i1}^{\prime},x_{i2}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{i,t-1}^{\prime})^{\prime},\ t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The matrix of instruments is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{i}=\mbox{diag}(x_{i1}^{o\prime},x_{i2}^{o\prime},\ldots,x_{i,T-1}^{o\prime})
\]

\end_inset

which has 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

 rows.
 It is fairly routine to apply GMM estimation with an optimal weighting
 matrix.
 With even moderate 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 there are lots of overidentifying restrictions (at least nominally).
 A simple strategy mentioned earlier is available: Estimate a reduced form
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 separately for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 So, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, run the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}^{o}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, and obtain the fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\Delta x_{it}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, estimate the FD equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y_{it}=\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

by pooled IV using instruments (not regressors) 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\Delta x_{it}}$
\end_inset

.
 This should probably be done even if using full GMM to confirm that the
 IVs are sufficiently correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Any of the IV approaches can suffer from a weak instrument problem when
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 has little correlation with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}^{o}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{it} & = & \omega_{t}+x_{i,t-1}+r_{it}\\
E(r_{it}|x_{i,t-1},x_{i,t-2},\ldots,x_{i0}) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\Delta x_{it}|x_{i,t-1}^{o})=E(\Delta x_{it})=\omega_{t}$
\end_inset

, and IV fails when a full set of year intercepts is included in the equation.
 If we add some assumptions, we can get more moment conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we assume dynamic completeness in the mean
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{it},y_{i,t-1},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,y_{i1},x_{i1},c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This condition rules out serial correlation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}\right\} $
\end_inset

, which is often too restrictive when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 does not include 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 (If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}\in x_{it}$
\end_inset

, there is no differene between sequential exogeneity and dynamic completeness.)
 Dynamic completeness means many more moment conditions are available.
 Using linear functions only, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=3,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\Delta u_{i,t-1}(c_{i}+u_{it})\right]=E\left[(\Delta y_{i,t-1}-\Delta x_{i,t-1}\beta)^{\prime}(y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta)\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

One drawback is that we often do not want to assume dynamic completeness.
 Plus, the extra conditions are nonlinear in parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Arellano and Bover (1995) suggested instead the restrictions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(\Delta x_{it}c_{i})=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which allows the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

level
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 to be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 but not the changes.
 It holds if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{it}=\omega_{t}+h_{i}+r_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ r_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This setup allows 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be arbitrarily correlated.
 However, the moment conditions would fail if, say,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{it}=\omega_{t}+h_{i}+g_{i}t+r_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated.
 To use the Arellano and Bover moment conditions, we need to let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

 to allow a nonzero mean.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\Delta x_{it}\left((c_{i}-\alpha)\right)+u_{it}\right]=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In terms of the parameters and observable data, we have the moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\Delta x_{it}(y_{it}-\alpha-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta)\right]=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can use these along with the moment conditions in the FD equation that
 are implied by sequential exogeneity.
 Note that all moment conditions are linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Because we are mixing moment conditions in FD and levels, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 includes year dummies (it should) then these must be differenced in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 The full set of moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
E\left[x_{i1}\left(\Delta y_{i2}-\Delta x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]\\
\vdots\\
E\left[x_{i,T-1}^{o}\left(\Delta y_{iT}-\Delta x_{iT}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]\\
E\left[\Delta x_{i2}\left(\Delta y_{i2}-\Delta x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]\\
\vdots\\
E\left[\Delta x_{iT}\left(\Delta y_{iT}-\Delta x_{iT}^{\prime}\beta\right)\right]
\end{array}\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We use GMM with a gneral weighting matrix to allow arbitrary correlation
 across al time periods/equations.
 (Now called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

system
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 GMM.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
AR(1) Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the simple AR(1) model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\rho y_{i,t-1}+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

Typically, the minimal assumpitons imposed are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{is}u_{it})=0,\ s=0,\ldots,t-1,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

(implied by dynamic completeness) so, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[y_{is}\left(\Delta y_{it}-\rho\Delta y-i,t-1\right)\right]=0,\ s\leq t-2
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Again, the model can suffer from weak instruments when 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is close to unity.
 Blundell and Bond (1998) showed that if the condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Cov}(\Delta y_{i1},c_{i})=\mbox{Cov}(y_{i1}-y_{i0},c_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

is added to 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|y_{i,t-1},\ldots,y_{i0},c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 then the Arellano and Bover extra moment conditions hold
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[\Delta y_{i,t-1}\left(y_{it}-\alpha-\rho y_{i,t-1}\right)\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

The condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Cov}(\Delta y_{i1},c_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

can be interpreted as a restriction on the initial condition, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i0}$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\rho\right|<1$
\end_inset

, write 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i0}$
\end_inset

 as a deviation from its steady state: 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i0}=\nicefrac{c_{i}}{(1-\rho)}+r_{i0}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the extra condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Cov}(r_{i0},c_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

the deviation of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i0}$
\end_inset

 from its steady state is uncorrelated with the SS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, let us consider extensions of the AR(1) model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\rho y_{i,t-1}+z_{it}^{\prime}\gamma+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

and use FD
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y_{it}=\rho\Delta y_{i,t-1}+\Delta z_{it}^{\prime}\gamma+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

We can use 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta z_{it}$
\end_inset

 as own IVs if they are strictly exogenous, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-h},\ h\geq2$
\end_inset

 and we can still add moment conditions in levels.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

 is incuded as a control, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}$
\end_inset

 (perhaps program assignment) is allowed to be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

 as well as with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ z_{it}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is not strictly exogenous, we can use 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ z_{i,t-1},\ldots,z_{i1}\right\} $
\end_inset

 as IVs, along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{i,t-2},\ldots,y_{i0}\right\} $
\end_inset

 in the FD equation at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 And, we still might use, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, the Arellano-Bover moments:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[\Delta y_{i,t-1}\left(y_{it}-\alpha-\rho y_{i,t-1}-z_{it}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\right] & = & 0\\
E\left[\Delta z_{it}\left(y_{it}-\alpha-\rho y_{i,t-1}-z_{it}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\right] & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

As usual, time dummies act as their own IVs.
\end_layout

